WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 5.9 · Internal Systems & Operations
Digital tools and the tech stack

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Choose tools after the process: for many new initiatives, a phone, chat and shared drive are enough.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:27.763
Your tech stack is the set of digital tools you use: email, files, messaging, accounting, contacts, planning, website and forms. For many new initiatives, a phone, a messaging app and a shared drive are enough.

4
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:28.763
Four checks for every tool

5
00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:38.981
Process first: Choose the tool after you know the process it will support. For example: The bakery maps its pre-order steps before choosing a simple online form.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:49.570
Fewer, connected: Prefer a few tools that connect over many that do not. For example: The bakery uses one shared calendar for orders instead of three separate apps.

7
00:00:49.570 --> 00:01:01.270
Owner and account: One named owner per tool, accounts in the initiative's name, and data you can export. For example: The bakery's email uses a shared address, not one founder's personal account.

8
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:14.170
Security and privacy: Two-step login, a password manager, and personal data kept only in suitable tools. For example: The bakery keeps trainees' details in a secure form, never in the group chat.

9
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.170
Why fewer is better

10
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:20.474
1. Each extra tool adds cost, training, logins and outdated information.

11
00:01:20.474 --> 00:01:26.148
2. Accounts in one founder's name are lost if that founder leaves.

12
00:01:26.148 --> 00:01:32.081
3. Data protection duties apply to every tool you choose.

13
00:01:32.481 --> 00:01:33.081
What it connects to

14
00:01:33.081 --> 00:01:37.644
1. Your operating map: the tool behind each system.

15
00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:42.578
2. Your information system: where files and access levels live.

16
00:01:42.578 --> 00:01:47.770
3. Your offboarding: removing access when someone leaves.

17
00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:48.770
What to avoid

18
00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:52.963
Buying apps before you know the process.

19
00:01:52.963 --> 00:01:58.267
Sharing passwords on a list instead of a password manager.

20
00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:04.570
Ignoring the cost of switching later when choosing free tiers.

21
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.970
You list your tools, their purpose, owner and risks.

22
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.970
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.570
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
